WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.015
Picture the counter of a post office.

2
00:00:05.015 --> 00:00:09.701
A customer walks up with a parcel and asks: how much to send this to Lisbon?

3
00:00:09.867 --> 00:00:12.774
The post office doesn't carry parcels that far.

4
00:00:12.774 --> 00:00:21.187
So it asks three courier companies, Northline, Eastgate and Fjord, and tells the customer the cheapest price within a third of a second.

5
00:00:21.333 --> 00:00:27.239
A program that does this is a quote service: it gives a price for a job before anyone books it.

6
00:00:27.239 --> 00:00:29.539
Over three sittings, we'll build one.

7
00:00:30.467 --> 00:00:37.683
A client, the program that asks, opens a connection to our server and sends one line of text: 7, Lisbon.

8
00:00:37.683 --> 00:00:40.251
The server answers with one line too.

9
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:41.479
Read it aloud.

10
00:00:41.479 --> 00:00:43.021
Request 7 went fine.

11
00:00:43.021 --> 00:00:48.804
Sending the parcel to Lisbon costs 12.42, and Northline offered that price.

12
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:55.382
The 7 is the client's own number for its question, a request id, and the server repeats it on the answer.

13
00:00:55.382 --> 00:00:58.117
It works like the stub you get for your coat.

14
00:00:58.117 --> 00:01:00.609
Show the number, get the right coat back.

15
00:01:00.609 --> 00:01:03.405
We'll see in a minute why the client needs it.

16
00:01:04.333 --> 00:01:07.749
The three sittings build the program from the outside in.

17
00:01:07.749 --> 00:01:12.543
This sitting builds the counter, on Netty, the Java library for network servers.

18
00:01:12.543 --> 00:01:15.958
The next one makes the phone calls to the three couriers.

19
00:01:15.958 --> 00:01:19.613
The last one keeps the service standing when a crowd arrives.

20
00:01:20.533 --> 00:01:23.218
Now, a question that's easy to be shy about.

21
00:01:23.218 --> 00:01:27.794
The clerk at the counter needs a price, and the price is a phone call away.

22
00:01:27.794 --> 00:01:30.662
A courier answers in about a tenth of a second.

23
00:01:30.662 --> 00:01:33.286
So why not just pick up the phone and wait?

24
00:01:33.286 --> 00:01:35.116
It's only a tenth of a second.

25
00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:40.318
Let's look at the clerk more closely.

26
00:01:40.318 --> 00:01:44.334
In this post office, each clerk owns a row of mailboxes, for good.

27
00:01:44.334 --> 00:01:49.201
Letters from many customers land in that row, and the clerk serves them in turn.

28
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:50.380
Let's map that.

29
00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:55.342
The clerk is an event loop: one thread that serves many connections in turn.

30
00:01:55.342 --> 00:01:59.652
Each mailbox is a channel, Netty's object for one open connection.

31
00:01:59.652 --> 00:02:02.525
And the phone call is our call to a courier.

32
00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:08.217
Ten customers send one question each, at the same moment, all into this clerk's row.

33
00:02:08.217 --> 00:02:13.951
Each price takes the courier a tenth of a second, and the clerk waits on the phone for each one.

34
00:02:13.951 --> 00:02:16.340
When does the last customer get a reply?

35
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:24.725
The clerk picks up the phone for the first letter and holds it until the price comes back.

36
00:02:24.725 --> 00:02:27.151
Only then does he open the second letter.

37
00:02:27.151 --> 00:02:28.039
Then the third.

38
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.570
Ten customers, ten phone calls, one after another.

39
00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:33.854
About a second for the last one.

40
00:02:33.854 --> 00:02:42.207
One run of the lesson's demo measured 1,163 milliseconds, and the courier never saw more than one question at a time.

41
00:02:42.933 --> 00:02:44.027
Now the other way.

42
00:02:44.027 --> 00:02:51.382
The clerk asks for the price, takes a ticket that says the answer will come later, and goes straight back to his letters.

43
00:02:51.382 --> 00:02:56.671
When a price arrives, it lands in his in-tray, and he writes the reply between letters.

44
00:02:56.867 --> 00:02:59.529
All ten questions are out at once.

45
00:02:59.529 --> 00:03:03.600
The same demo answered everyone in 146 milliseconds.

46
00:03:04.533 --> 00:03:05.888
And here's the trap.

47
00:03:05.888 --> 00:03:13.272
The first version passes every test with one customer, which is exactly how many customers a simple test has.

48
00:03:13.467 --> 00:03:14.854
So the point is this.

49
00:03:14.854 --> 00:03:18.355
Waiting looks harmless until customers share a clerk.

50
00:03:18.355 --> 00:03:21.657
Then one customer's wait becomes everybody's wait.

51
00:03:22.533 --> 00:03:25.977
That gives the one rule every part of this service obeys.

52
00:03:25.977 --> 00:03:27.608
The event loop never waits.

53
00:03:27.608 --> 00:03:32.260
Hand the ticket back, and finish the job on the loop when the answer arrives.

54
00:03:33.133 --> 00:03:34.630
Here our picture breaks.

55
00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:38.872
A real post office would notice the queue and open a second counter.

56
00:03:38.872 --> 00:03:41.056
Netty never adds a loop on its own.

57
00:03:41.056 --> 00:03:44.424
It doesn't even notice that the clerk is on the phone.

58
00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:50.350
And if you add a second clerk yourself, each one serves five customers, still on the phone.

59
00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:52.561
About half a second instead of one.

60
00:03:52.561 --> 00:03:54.457
More clerks divide the damage.

61
00:03:54.457 --> 00:03:55.784
They don't remove it.

62
00:03:56.667 --> 00:03:59.132
Two more details are there on purpose.

63
00:03:59.132 --> 00:04:02.376
First, the server reads at most 64 bytes per line.

64
00:04:02.533 --> 00:04:11.371
Without a limit, a client that sends bytes and never ends the line would make the server keep every one of them, waiting for a line that never ends.

65
00:04:11.371 --> 00:04:14.835
A longer line gets an error, and the connection is closed.

66
00:04:15.533 --> 00:04:17.291
Second, the request id.

67
00:04:17.291 --> 00:04:21.722
One client sends two questions: 7, Lisbon, then 8, Dublin.

68
00:04:21.722 --> 00:04:24.167
Dublin's price comes back first.

69
00:04:24.167 --> 00:04:26.077
Which reply leaves first?

70
00:04:28.933 --> 00:04:29.597
Dublin's.

71
00:04:29.597 --> 00:04:35.351
A reply leaves when its price arrives, so a quick answer overtakes a slow one.

72
00:04:35.351 --> 00:04:37.342
8, OK, Dublin, 9.80, Fjord.

73
00:04:37.342 --> 00:04:38.449
Then 7, Lisbon.

74
00:04:38.449 --> 00:04:42.727
The client matches them by their numbers, like coat stubs.

75
00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:47.633
Here's the whole counter as a line of stations, called a pipeline.

76
00:04:47.633 --> 00:04:55.453
Every message on a channel passes through it, and each station is a handler, a small object that reacts to the channel's events.

77
00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:59.871
The frame station cuts the bytes into lines, at most 64 bytes each.

78
00:04:59.871 --> 00:05:04.970
The decoder turns a line into text, and replies go back out through the encoder.

79
00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:08.030
Flow control gets its own section of the lesson.

80
00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:11.663
And the last station, QuoteHandler, answers the question.

81
00:05:11.663 --> 00:05:13.831
That's where the phone call hides.

82
00:05:14.733 --> 00:05:18.279
In code, the clerk on the phone is one innocent word: join.

83
00:05:18.279 --> 00:05:22.427
It waits for the ticket's answer and holds the thread while it waits.

84
00:05:22.427 --> 00:05:27.836
The fix asks for the price and says what to do when it arrives, on the channel's own loop.

85
00:05:27.836 --> 00:05:29.459
Kotlin says the same thing.

86
00:05:30.333 --> 00:05:36.961
So look in your own code for join, get, or runBlocking inside anything that runs on an event loop.

87
00:05:36.961 --> 00:05:39.328
Each one is the clerk on the phone.

88
00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.755
So, why not just wait a tenth of a second?

89
00:05:42.755 --> 00:05:45.370
Because that tenth of a second isn't yours.

90
00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:47.925
It belongs to everyone in the clerk's row.

91
00:05:47.925 --> 00:05:54.008
The counter hands each question back as a ticket, numbers its replies, and never picks up the phone.

92
00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:55.060
Quick check.

93
00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:57.928
One client sends 9, Oslo, then 10, Rome.

94
00:05:57.928 --> 00:05:59.863
Rome's price arrives first.

95
00:05:59.863 --> 00:06:04.595
Which reply leaves first, and how does the client tell them apart?

96
00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:09.638
The reply for 10 leaves first.

97
00:06:09.638 --> 00:06:15.152
The client reads the number at the front of each reply and matches it to its own question.
